Deferred CPU-request ordering checks
===================================

Run from this source tree, without building or booting a kernel:

  sh tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/cpu_work_test.sh
  CC=gcc CFLAGS=-O2 sh tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/cpu_work_test.sh
  CC=clang CFLAGS='-O1 -g -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=all -fno-omit-frame-pointer' \
    sh tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/cpu_work_test.sh

The runner extracts verbatim CPU receive handlers, workers, queue submission
and detachment, hotplug init and cleanup. It tests real admission, error/free
paths and ACK fields against controlled scheduler, CPU mutation, registration
and transport boundaries. A legal adverse scheduler runs unordered requests
newest-first; the ordered-queue boundary follows the kernel's FIFO contract.
This is not a real Linux workqueue or concurrent hotplug execution test.

The lock boundary also models hard-IPI context with PREEMPT_RT semantics:
ordinary spin locks may sleep, raw locks may not. Before the review fix, that
check reported 12 invalid lock acquisitions (44 checks, one failure). It passes
with raw submission/detachment/publication locks. This models the API choice;
it is not a reproduced PREEMPT_RT kernel failure or full RT qualification.

Before the source fix: 35 checks, 14 failures, including a remove ACK preceding
an older queued add and the CPU becoming online afterward. Queue initialization,
registration failure, sparse full-width CPU IDs, worker errors, allocation
failure, short payloads, teardown admission and draining are also exercised.
Cleanup injects an already-entered callback at unregister to check that it
cannot submit after the CPU queue is detached. Kernel callback-object lifetime
and actual IRQ synchronization are not simulated or qualified by that check.

Behavior and limits
-------------------

Incoming CPU adds/removes now share alloc_ordered_workqueue, which guarantees
at most one running work item and FIFO queue order. Do not substitute a bound
queue with max_active=1: the in-tree workqueue API documents why that is not a
global ordering guarantee. A remove worker and its ACK follow CPU requests
already admitted ahead of it. The separate ack_test retains real pending/ID
and worker echo coverage with its single-item scheduler boundary.

Submission and queue detachment share an IRQ-safe raw spinlock. Detach closes CPU
admission before unregister; destroy_workqueue drains admitted CPU work before
freeing the queue. Init failure does not publish a resource handler without its
CPU queue. Rejected work is freed and returns -ESHUTDOWN to the receive dispatch;
no success ACK is fabricated. The existing dispatcher logs that error rather
than replying, so a requester may time out and must retain uncertain ownership.

This orders already received CPU requests only. It does not cancel a timed-out
request, exclude later messages or another controller, serialize local pool
operations with all incoming workers, drain memory/device work, or fence all
surviving peers. Memory/device and shutdown scheduling stay unchanged. No wire
layout or request-ID change is introduced.

Full halt/HALTED/force-halt settlement, destruction/admission, callback and
ring/image lifetime, shutdown quiescence and cross-kernel dependencies remain
release gates. All parked CPUs or a reset ring alone are not a queued-work
cancellation proof. Kernel lockdep, x86 runtime/concurrency, QEMU/OpenSBI and
same/different-Image 50-cycle failure/stress tests remain required.
